<Base
  title="48 Hours in Big Sur | 48 Hours in California"
  description="A relaxed 48-hour Big Sur itinerary with Highway 1, Bixby Creek Bridge, redwoods, McWay Falls, and time to experience the California coast without turning it into a checklist."
>
  <article>
    <h1>48 Hours in Big Sur</h1>

    <figure>
      <img
        src="/images/big-sur-highway-1-hero.jpg"
        alt="Highway 1 winding along the rugged Big Sur coastline above the Pacific Ocean"
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      <strong>
        Two days of cliffs, redwoods, Highway 1, and the rare California
        landscape that makes getting somewhere less important than the road
        between.
      </strong>
    </p>

    <p>Big Sur isn't really a town.</p>

    <p>It isn't a single park either.</p>

    <p>
      It's a stretch of California coast where the Santa Lucia Mountains rise
      abruptly beside the Pacific and Highway 1 finds its way between them.
    </p>

    <p>That's important because Big Sur requires a different kind of 48-hour trip.</p>

    <p>
      There isn't a downtown where we'll park the car and spend the afternoon
      wandering between shops and restaurants.
    </p>

    <p>There isn't one attraction we're trying to reach.</p>

    <p>Here, the road is part of the destination.</p>

    <p>You'll round a curve and see the Pacific hundreds of feet below.</p>

    <p>
      Ten minutes later, you may be surrounded by redwoods beside the Big Sur
      River.
    </p>

    <p>
      Another bend reveals cliffs, another bridge, another canyon, another
      stretch of coastline disappearing into the distance.
    </p>

    <p>The temptation is to stop at every turnout.</p>

    <p>Don't.</p>

    <p>
      Big Sur becomes surprisingly exhausting when every beautiful view becomes
      another appointment.
    </p>

    <p>We're going to choose carefully.</p>

    <p>
      <strong>
        Big Sur isn't a collection of stops along Highway 1. Highway 1 through
        Big Sur is the experience connecting them.
      </strong>
    </p>

    <h2>At a Glance</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>Best for:</strong> Couples, photographers, hikers, nature lovers,
      road-trip travelers, and anyone who occasionally wants scenery to be the
      entertainment.
    </p>

    <p><strong>The pace:</strong> Slow and deliberately flexible.</p>

    <p><strong>Stay:</strong> Two nights.</p>

    <p>
      <strong>Don't miss:</strong> Highway 1 itself, Bixby Creek Bridge, the
      redwood and river landscape, the central Big Sur coastline, and at least
      one moment when you simply stop somewhere beautiful.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Good to know:</strong> Highway conditions, park access, trails,
      wildfire impacts, weather, and coastal conditions can change. Big Sur is
      one destination where checking conditions shortly before departure is
      essential.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to Stay</h2>

    <p>If your budget and availability allow it, <strong>stay in Big Sur itself</strong>.</p>

    <p>
      There are campgrounds, cabins, inns, lodges, and some exceptionally
      expensive resorts.
    </p>

    <p>The particular property matters less to our itinerary than its location.</p>

    <p>
      Staying within Big Sur lets you experience the area before and after many
      day visitors have left.
    </p>

    <p>
      It also means you don't spend the beginning and end of each day commuting
      from Monterey or somewhere farther north.
    </p>

    <p>That matters here.</p>

    <p>
      An evening when you no longer need to drive Highway 1 is part of the
      experience.
    </p>

    <p>
      Big Sur lodging can fill well ahead of time, particularly during popular
      travel periods, so this isn't a destination where I'd depend on finding
      something after arriving.
    </p>

    <p>Reserve the room.</p>

    <p>Then build the trip around it.</p>
    <h3>Check Big Sur Hotel Availability</h3>

    <p>
      Compare current lodging options and availability for your travel dates.

    </p>
    <div class="eg-widget" data-widget="search" data-program="us-expedia" data-lobs="stays" data-network="pz" data-camref="1100l6uuC8" data-pubref=""></div>
    <script class="eg-widgets-script" src="https://creator.expediagroup.com/products/widgets/assets/eg-widgets.js"></script>

    <h2>Day 1 — Enter Big Sur Slowly</h2>

    <p>Approach from the north.</p>

    <p>As you leave the Carmel area behind, watch the landscape change.</p>

    <p>Development begins thinning.</p>

    <p>The mountains move closer.</p>

    <p>Highway 1 begins clinging more tightly to the coastline.</p>

    <p>
      And eventually you'll realize that getting somewhere has stopped being
      the most interesting part of the drive.
    </p>

    <p>That's Big Sur announcing itself.</p>

    <h3>Bixby Creek Bridge</h3>

    <figure>
      <img
        src="/images/big-sur-bixby-creek-bridge.jpg"
        alt="Bixby Creek Bridge spanning a coastal canyon along Highway 1 in Big Sur"
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      Make <strong>Bixby Creek Bridge</strong> one of your first deliberate
      stops.
    </p>

    <p>It's one of California's most recognizable bridges for good reason.</p>

    <p>But this doesn't need to become a lengthy attraction.</p>

    <p>Find a safe, legal place to stop.</p>

    <p>Walk to an appropriate viewpoint.</p>

    <p>Look at the bridge, the canyon, the cliffs, and the Pacific.</p>

    <p>Take the photograph.</p>

    <p>Then put the camera down for a minute.</p>

    <p>Look again.</p>

    <p>And continue south.</p>

    <h3>Don't Stop Everywhere</h3>

    <p>This may be the most useful advice in our entire Big Sur itinerary.</p>

    <p>There will be another turnout.</p>

    <p>There will be another cliff.</p>

    <p>There will be another extraordinary ocean view.</p>

    <p>You don't need all of them.</p>

    <p>Choose some.</p>

    <p>Pass others.</p>

    <p>If a particular place makes you want to stop, stop.</p>

    <p>
      But don't allow every turnout to become an obligation simply because
      someone marked it on a map.
    </p>

    <p>We're not collecting viewpoints.</p>

    <p>We're traveling through a landscape.</p>

    <h3>Afternoon — From Cliffs to Redwoods</h3>

    <p>Continue into the Big Sur River corridor.</p>

    <p>
      This is one of the transitions that makes Big Sur particularly memorable.
    </p>

    <p>
      The exposed coastal landscape begins giving way to trees, river country,
      and redwoods.
    </p>

    <p>
      Under normal operating conditions, <strong>Pfeiffer Big Sur State Park</strong>
      is one of the places where visitors can experience this inland side of Big
      Sur.
    </p>

    <p>
      The park lies along the Big Sur River on the western slope of the Santa
      Lucia Mountains and is known for its redwood environment and trails.
    </p>

    <p>An important distinction:</p>

    <p><strong>Pfeiffer Big Sur State Park is not an oceanfront park.</strong></p>

    <p>That's precisely why it belongs in our itinerary.</p>

    <p>We've spent much of the day looking at the Pacific.</p>

    <p>Now Big Sur shows us something else.</p>

    <p>
      Choose a walk or trail appropriate to current conditions, available time,
      and your ability.
    </p>

    <p>We aren't prescribing one mandatory hike because trail access can change.</p>

    <p>If a short walk beneath the trees is enough, let it be enough.</p>

    <h3>Late Afternoon — Check In</h3>

    <p>Now stop traveling.</p>

    <p>Check into your lodging.</p>

    <p>Unload the car.</p>

    <p>Sit somewhere.</p>

    <p>Walk around the property.</p>

    <p>Listen.</p>

    <p>Big Sur gets quieter after the day's traffic begins thinning.</p>

    <p>Give yourself an opportunity to notice.</p>

    <h3>Evening — Stay Nearby</h3>

    <p>Have dinner at your lodging or somewhere reasonably close.</p>

    <p>
      Don't spend the evening driving thirty miles because a restaurant farther
      down Highway 1 has better online reviews.
    </p>

    <p>Darkness changes Highway 1.</p>

    <p>So do fog and weather.</p>

    <p>And you already spent much of the day on the road.</p>

    <p>Tonight we're staying close.</p>

    <p>If there's somewhere outside to sit safely afterward, do that.</p>

    <p>Big Sur doesn't need much help entertaining you.</p>

    <h2>Day 2 — Follow Highway 1 South</h2>

    <p>Start relatively early.</p>

    <p>Today we're going deeper into the Big Sur coastline.</p>

    <p>But again, we aren't measuring success by mileage.</p>

    <p>The objective isn't to drive as far south as possible.</p>

    <p>The objective is to experience the coast.</p>

    <h3>Morning — Julia Pfeiffer Burns and McWay Falls</h3>

    <figure>
      <img
        src="/images/big-sur-mcway-falls.jpg"
        alt="McWay Falls dropping toward a protected coastal cove in Big Sur"
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      Under normal access conditions, make
      <strong>Julia Pfeiffer Burns State Park</strong> one of the day's principal
      stops.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is home to <strong>McWay Falls</strong>, where water drops from the
      cliffs toward the protected cove below.
    </p>

    <p>
      View it from the designated trail and overlook areas that are open during
      your visit.
    </p>

    <p>Do not attempt to reach the beach.</p>

    <p>
      There is no public beach access at McWay Falls, and closed areas beyond
      barriers exist for safety and resource protection.
    </p>

    <p>This gives us an important Big Sur rule:</p>

    <p><strong>A beautiful photograph isn't permission to ignore a boundary.</strong></p>

    <p>
      Big Sur contains steep cliffs, unstable terrain, powerful surf, protected
      habitat, and changing conditions.
    </p>

    <p>Respecting barriers isn't interfering with the experience.</p>

    <p>It's part of experiencing this landscape responsibly.</p>

    <h3>Continue South — But Not Automatically</h3>

    <p>
      After McWay Falls, continue south if current Highway 1 conditions,
      weather, and your own interest make sense.
    </p>

    <p>Stop selectively.</p>

    <p>Maybe there's a viewpoint that catches your attention.</p>

    <p>
      Maybe current park conditions give you an opportunity for another coastal
      walk.
    </p>

    <p>
      Maybe you've already seen enough from the car and would rather spend more
      time in one place.
    </p>

    <p>All are legitimate choices.</p>

    <p>This is where we're intentionally leaving space in the itinerary.</p>

    <p>Big Sur changes.</p>

    <p>Road conditions change.</p>

    <p>Trail conditions change.</p>

    <p>Weather changes.</p>

    <p>Your energy changes.</p>

    <p>The itinerary needs to accommodate all four.</p>

    <h3>Choose One Walk</h3>

    <p>
      If you want more hiking today, choose
      <strong>one trail based on current conditions</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Don't arrive committed to a particular hike you read about six months
      earlier.
    </p>

    <p>Check what's open.</p>

    <p>Consider the weather.</p>

    <p>Consider how much daylight remains.</p>

    <p>
      And consider whether you actually want a substantial hike or simply
      another hour outside.
    </p>

    <p>The longest trail isn't automatically the best experience.</p>

    <p>
      Sometimes a short coastal walk followed by an hour looking at the Pacific
      is exactly right.
    </p>

    <h3>Lunch — Keep It Simple</h3>

    <p>
      Have lunch somewhere convenient to your route, or carry what you need if
      that's appropriate for your plans.
    </p>

    <p>
      Big Sur isn't the place to build the day around driving to a specific
      restaurant at a specific minute.
    </p>

    <p>Food matters.</p>

    <p>But flexibility matters more.</p>

    <p>And carry water.</p>

    <h3>Afternoon — Turn Around</h3>

    <p>At some point, turn north.</p>

    <p>That may sound anticlimactic.</p>

    <p>It isn't.</p>

    <p>
      One of the pleasures of Highway 1 is discovering that driving the same
      stretch in the opposite direction doesn't necessarily feel like repeating
      it.
    </p>

    <p>The perspective changes.</p>

    <p>The light changes.</p>

    <p>The coastline unfolds differently.</p>

    <p>And the Pacific now appears from another angle.</p>

    <p>Remember some of those turnouts you passed this morning?</p>

    <p>Now you can choose one.</p>

    <p>Stop.</p>

    <p>Take your time.</p>

    <p>Then continue north.</p>

    <h3>Late Afternoon — Stop Driving</h3>

    <p>
      Return to your lodging area early enough that the day doesn't end with
      you arriving exhausted after dark.
    </p>

    <p>This afternoon deliberately has no major attraction.</p>

    <p>Sit near the river.</p>

    <p>Return to the redwoods.</p>

    <p>Have coffee.</p>

    <p>Read.</p>

    <p>Walk.</p>

    <p>Take a nap.</p>

    <p>Find somewhere safe to watch the light change.</p>

    <p>
      The freedom to stop moving is one of the reasons we stayed in Big Sur
      rather than simply driving through it.
    </p>

    <p>
      By now, the road that seemed dramatic and unfamiliar when you arrived has
      begun acquiring landmarks.
    </p>

    <p>Big Sur has started becoming a place rather than simply a scenic highway.</p>

    <h3>Evening — One More Big Sur Night</h3>

    <p>Have dinner.</p>

    <p>Talk about the places that surprised you.</p>

    <p>Maybe it was McWay Falls.</p>

    <p>Maybe Bixby Bridge.</p>

    <p>Maybe a redwood.</p>

    <p>
      Maybe it was an unnamed turnout where you spent twenty minutes watching
      waves hit the cliffs.
    </p>

    <p>
      That last possibility is exactly why we've resisted overplanning this
      trip.
    </p>

    <p>Tomorrow morning, don't rush out.</p>

    <p>Have breakfast.</p>

    <p>Walk outside.</p>

    <p>
      Look at the trees or mountains or Pacific—whatever your lodging gives you.
    </p>

    <p>Then begin the drive home or toward your next California destination.</p>

    <p>Unless you have another three hours.</p>

    <h2>If You Have 3 More Hours</h2>

    <h3>Point Sur</h3>

    <p>Consider <strong>Point Sur State Historic Park</strong>.</p>

    <p>
      The dramatic volcanic rock rising beside Highway 1 is difficult to miss,
      but the historic lighthouse above it provides a completely different
      experience from our other Big Sur stops.
    </p>

    <p>
      Access is generally through scheduled guided tours rather than simply
      wandering onto the property, so this extension requires more planning than
      one of our scenic overlooks.
    </p>

    <p>Check the current tour schedule before building it into your trip.</p>

    <p>
      If the timing works, it adds history, maritime life, and a human story to
      a weekend dominated by landscape.
    </p>

    <p>If it doesn't work, don't force it.</p>

    <p>Big Sur has already given you plenty.</p>

    <h2>If You Have One More Day</h2>

    <h3>Point Lobos and the Transition Toward Carmel</h3>

    <p>Head north toward <strong>Point Lobos State Natural Reserve</strong>.</p>

    <p>
      Point Lobos deserves more time than we wanted to steal from our core Big
      Sur itinerary.
    </p>

    <p>
      Its headlands, coves, coastal trails, wildlife, and views provide a
      wonderful transition between Big Sur's dramatic coastline and
      Carmel-by-the-Sea.
    </p>

    <p>Spend several hours walking rather than racing through.</p>

    <p>Then continue toward Carmel.</p>

    <p>
      This also illustrates something important about our growing 48 Hours in
      California collection:
    </p>

    <p>Destinations don't always end at a municipal boundary.</p>

    <p>Sometimes one landscape gradually introduces the next.</p>

    <p>Big Sur leads naturally toward Carmel and Monterey.</p>

    <p>We'll let those places tell their own stories.</p>

    <h2>Before You Go</h2>

    <p>
      Big Sur requires more preparation than many of our other 48-hour
      destinations.
    </p>

    <p><strong>First, check Highway 1 conditions shortly before departure.</strong></p>

    <p>Not only when you book the trip.</p>

    <p>Not only a week beforehand.</p>

    <p>Check again before you leave.</p>

    <p>
      Road work, slides, fires, weather, and other events can change access and
      travel times.
    </p>

    <p>Check current California State Parks conditions too.</p>

    <p>
      A trail or park area that was open when this guide was written may not be
      open when you arrive.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Download offline maps.</strong></p>

    <p>Cell service can be extremely limited or nonexistent in portions of Big Sur.</p>

    <p>
      Don't depend on your phone suddenly finding a signal when you need
      directions.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Fuel the car before entering the more remote portions of the coast.</strong></p>

    <p>Don't make searching for gasoline part of the adventure.</p>

    <p>Carry water.</p>

    <p>Bring food or snacks appropriate to your plans.</p>

    <p>Wear shoes suitable for the walks you intend to take.</p>

    <p>Bring layers.</p>

    <p>Coastal weather can change quickly.</p>

    <p>
      And avoid building a schedule so precise that a twenty-minute delay ruins
      the afternoon.
    </p>

    <p>Big Sur doesn't operate according to your itinerary.</p>

    <p>Fog arrives.</p>

    <p>Road work happens.</p>

    <p>Parking fills.</p>

    <p>A trail closes.</p>

    <p>
      Or perhaps you find a viewpoint so beautiful that you decide to stay
      there much longer than expected.
    </p>

    <p>That last one isn't a problem.</p>

    <p>It's the reason you came.</p>

    <p>Most importantly, drive carefully.</p>

    <p>Highway 1 demands attention.</p>

    <p>
      If you want to admire the scenery, use a safe, legal turnout and stop the
      car.
    </p>

    <p>
      The person driving shouldn't be trying to experience Big Sur through the
      side window.
    </p>

    <p>And when you do stop, don't feel compelled to photograph everything.</p>

    <p>Some landscapes are allowed to exist without becoming content.</p>

    <p>
      <strong>
        Big Sur isn't a collection of stops along Highway 1. Highway 1 through
        Big Sur is the experience connecting them.
      </strong>
    </p>

    <p>And remember what these trips are supposed to be:</p>

    <p><strong>48 hours away—not 48 hours of appointments.</strong></p>

    <h2>Related Getaways</h2>

    <p>
      Continue north with <strong>48 Hours in Carmel-by-the-Sea</strong>, where
      Big Sur's wild coastline gives way to a walkable village, distinctive
      architecture, restaurants, beaches, and another very different
      relationship with the Pacific.
    </p>

    <p>
      Nearby <strong>48 Hours in Monterey</strong> adds California history,
      Cannery Row, Monterey Bay, marine life, and one of the state's most
      recognizable coastal communities.
    </p>

    <p>
      Farther south, <strong>48 Hours in Cambria</strong> continues the Highway
      1 story with a smaller coastal town, Moonstone Beach, and a much gentler
      pace.
    </p>

    <p>
      And farther inland, <strong>48 Hours in Paso Robles</strong> exchanges
      ocean cliffs for vineyards, ranchland, and wine-country roads.
    </p>

    <p>Big Sur is different from all of them.</p>

    <p>There isn't much of a town to explore.</p>

    <p>There isn't a long restaurant list to complete.</p>

    <p>There isn't even one attraction that explains why people come.</p>

    <p>The explanation is outside the windshield.</p>

    <p>Mountains.</p>

    <p>Redwoods.</p>

    <p>Cliffs.</p>

    <p>Ocean.</p>

    <p>Highway.</p>

    <p>
      And enough empty space between them to remind us that sometimes the best
      part of traveling somewhere is simply <strong>being on the way.</strong>
    </p>

